{
 "item": "E3",
 "where": "Theorem 4.22",
 "paper text": [
  {
   "where": "Section 4.4",
   "text": "Suppose every bridge with at least two interior vertices has\nthree attachments and a planar relevant completion. The\npossible bridges are then those with one interior vertex and\nthe forms in Proposition~\\ref{prop:three-root-disjoint}.\nAt most three four-attachment bridges have the same attachment\nset, since four would give $K_{4,4}$ and hence a $K_5$ minor.\nWe combine these bridges with disjoint interiors, subject to\nthe attachment restrictions and Lemma~\\ref{dis:counts}.\nA bridge containing $P_m$ includes that path and all\n$\\Psi_1$-bridges meeting its interior, so its form also\naccounts for the length of $P_m$. These enumerations are\nrecorded in~\\supp{E3,~E4}. The next lemma says that if a bridge with three attachments,\ntogether with four vertices having those attachments as their\nneighborhood, is toroidal, then adding some two edges between\nits attachments gives a planar graph."
  },
  {
   "where": "Theorem 4.22 (proof)",
   "text": "These give $72$ labeled forms. Among the eighteen triples meeting\nboth $A_1,A_2$, twelve contain an edge $P_i$, from three choices of\nthat edge and four choices of the third vertex. The other six\ncontain none, giving $12\\cdot3+6\\cdot6=72$. Form graphs by adding\nthese bridges to $\\Psi_1$ with disjoint interiors, using at most\nthree on any one triple by Lemma~\\ref{dis:counts} and at most five\nin total. By Lemma~\\ref{dis:four-root-isolation}, there is at most\none four-attachment vertex on each set $\\{s_i,t_i,s_j,t_j\\}$.\nWe also allow any subset of the six edges $s_it_j$ with $i\\ne j$.\nWe retain only graphs satisfying\nLemma~\\ref{lem:independent-neighborhood}(a) and\nLemma~\\ref{dis:four-root-isolation}, and exclude disjoint\n$s_i$--$t_j$ and $s_j$--$t_i$ paths through distinct bridges by\nLemma~\\ref{dis:opposite-order}. The obstruction check in~\\supp{E3}\nleaves only $\\mathfrak T_{48}$ and $\\mathfrak T_{58}$. Neither graph\nhas a four-attachment bridge. Together with $\\mathfrak T_5$,\n$\\mathfrak T_{68}$ and $\\mathfrak T_{69}$, these give the graphs in\nthe statement.\n\\end{proof}"
  }
 ],
 "vertex names": "Psi_1 with twins p, q on {s1, s2, s3} and r, z on {t1, t2, t3}; the paths are the edges s1t1, s2t2, s3t3. Interior vertices of copy k of atom A<i> are named A<i>#k@<role>.",
